WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
In this scenario, Marcus is seriously ill and asks whether his

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.980
life policy can provide money while he is living.

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.940
An accelerated benefit pays an eligible amount early under the policy's

4
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.640
qualifying conditions and reduces the death benefit otherwise payable.

5
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.920
It is not automatically an extra benefit on top of the

6
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.420
full original death protection.

7
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.420
It also differs from borrowing against cash value or selling a

8
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.480
policy to an outside settlement provider.

9
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.080
Identify the actual feature first.

10
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.960
Maryland's accelerated-benefit chapter requires terminal illness among

11
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.400
its qualifying events for products within that chapter.

12
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.560
It does not mean every illness automatically qualifies for payment.

13
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.960
A contract may describe a limited life expectancy, a condition requiring

14
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.980
extraordinary intervention, or expected permanent confinement in an eligible

15
00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:00.160
institution. These are specific triggers, not interchangeable labels for

16
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.880
any hospital visit. A short rehabilitation stay is not automatically

17
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.480
confinement expected to last for the rest of the insured's life.

18
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.900
Some approved designs include chronic-illness conditions involving daily

19
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.640
activities or severe cognitive impairment.

20
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:21.160
Read the required certification, degree of assistance, permanence and other

21
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.180
conditions. A family member's description of being very sick is important

22
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.180
context, but it does not replace the medical evidence required by

23
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.560
the actual benefit provision.

24
00:01:31.339 --> 00:01:35.979
Activities of daily living include bathing, dressing, eating, toileting and

25
00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:40.279
transferring. Continence, meaning control of bladder and bowel function, is

26
00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:45.179
also included. Transferring means moving between positions such as a bed

27
00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:49.879
and a chair. Shopping, driving and managing investments are not substitutes

28
00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:51.199
for those named activities.

29
00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:55.879
Suppose a fictional rider requires permanent inability to perform two

30
00:01:55.879 --> 00:01:59.159
specified activities without substantial assistance.

31
00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:04.079
A physician's evidence of qualifying help with bathing and dressing addresses

32
00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:08.679
two named activities, but the remaining rider conditions still must be

33
00:02:08.679 --> 00:02:13.379
met. Do not turn that example into a universal two-activity rule

34
00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:15.399
for every acceleration product.

35
00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:20.399
A separate cognitive-impairment trigger can require substantial supervision

36
00:02:20.399 --> 00:02:21.839
under the actual wording.

37
00:02:22.723 --> 00:02:26.603
Assume a two hundred thousand dollar death benefit and an approved

38
00:02:26.603 --> 00:02:29.403
acceleration of one hundred thousand dollars of that benefit.

39
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:34.783
In this simplified example, a stated ten thousand dollar discount reduces

40
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:37.923
the immediate cash payment to ninety thousand dollars.

41
00:02:38.623 --> 00:02:43.229
The remaining death benefit is one hundred thousand dollars, because one

42
00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.243
hundred thousand of the original benefit was accelerated.

43
00:02:47.163 --> 00:02:50.283
It is not one hundred ten thousand merely because the owner

44
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:52.503
received ninety thousand cash.

45
00:02:53.343 --> 00:02:57.943
Real contracts can use different charges, discounts or lien methods.

46
00:02:58.863 --> 00:03:02.903
Read the insurer's before-and-after statement and any outstanding debt.

47
00:03:03.543 --> 00:03:08.603
Never infer remaining protection by subtracting only the

48
00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:10.483
check from the old face amount.

49
00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.920
The death-benefit change is only one part of the calculation.

50
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.900
Acceleration can affect cash value, accumulation accounts, outstanding loans,

51
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.320
future loan access and premium obligations.

52
00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.280
Maryland's actuarial rule addresses proportional cash-value reduction.

53
00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.080
An alternative approach places a lien against the policy, with permitted

54
00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.560
interest that can reduce value available later.

55
00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.060
Do not promise that all existing policy debt disappears or that

56
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.400
every premium stops. At the time the benefit is claimed, the

57
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.680
insurer must explain continuing premium requirements needed to keep the

58
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.440
policy in force. Marcus needs those continuing obligations in writing

59
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.460
so he can understand what coverage and resources remain after the

60
00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:02.028
payment. For an agent-solicited application under Maryland's rule, the

61
00:04:02.028 --> 00:04:06.148
written benefit description and effects disclosure is provided before or with

62
00:04:06.148 --> 00:04:10.148
the application and acknowledged by the applicant and writing agent.

63
00:04:10.808 --> 00:04:14.208
Additional information is required when acceleration is requested.

64
00:04:15.048 --> 00:04:20.008
Tax treatment requires its own analysis: qualifying terminal-illness benefits

65
00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:25.148
can be excluded from federal income, while chronic-illness exclusions have

66
00:04:25.148 --> 00:04:29.848
additional rules. Do not promise every payment is tax-free or that

67
00:04:29.848 --> 00:04:31.988
government-benefit eligibility is unaffected.

68
00:04:32.908 --> 00:04:36.408
The insurer must provide the required tax and benefit-eligibility

69
00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:41.668
disclosures. The person holding an assignment, or an irrevocable beneficiary,

70
00:04:41.668 --> 00:04:44.448
may also need to provide the required concurrence.

71
00:04:45.048 --> 00:04:50.968
Review the trigger, calculation and continuing obligations together before

72
00:04:50.968 --> 00:04:52.868
treating the transaction as understood.
